## Authority Statement

### Source of Authority

This document derives its authority from the System Objective Specification (SOS), which is the governing specification for all system objectives within the Adaptive Vehicle Intelligence Architecture (AVIA).

The SOS delegates the governance, management, and control of system objectives to this document.

---

### Scope of Authority

This document governs the lifecycle and management of all system objectives contained within the Objectives subdomain.

Its authority includes, but is not limited to:

- Objective classification.
- Objective identification.
- Objective creation.
- Objective modification.
- Objective approval.
- Objective version control.
- Objective traceability.
- Objective retirement.
- Compliance requirements for objective documentation.

---

### Authority Boundaries

This document does not:

- Modify or replace the Project Charter.
- Modify or replace the System Objective Specification (SOS).
- Define system requirements.
- Define engineering architectures.
- Define verification or validation procedures.
- Define the technical content of individual objectives.

Those responsibilities remain with their respective governing documents.

---

### Compliance Requirement

All objective category documents and every individual system objective developed within the AVIA repository shall comply with the governance principles and requirements established in this document.

Non-compliance shall require formal engineering review before approval.

---

### Delegated Authority

Authority flows through the AVIA engineering hierarchy as follows:

Project Charter
→ System Objective Specification (SOS)
→ Objective Governance
→ Objective Category Documents
→ Individual System Objectives

Each level governs the level directly beneath it while remaining accountable to the level above.

---

### Engineering Perspective

The Objective Governance document exists to ensure that all system objectives are created, managed, reviewed, and maintained under a consistent engineering framework.

Governance within AVIA is based on explicit authority, complete traceability, disciplined change management, and solution-independent systems engineering.
